Let an open window take a new gesture tuning.

What: `Window::set_gesture_tuning(GestureTuning)` is public (it was a test-only helper that rebuilt the touch
recognizer) and now changes the tuning in place through `TouchGestureRecognizer::set_tuning`: touches in progress
keep their state and are judged by the new values from the next event on. It passes the tuning on to the platform
window: `PlatformWindow::set_gesture_tuning`, a no-op by default; gpui_web implements it, so its keyboard tap
shadow (opened with `WindowParams::gesture_tuning`) clears at the new touch slop, as the recognizer does.
`Window::gesture_tuning()` is public too, so a caller can retune one value of an open window's tuning
(`GestureTuning { touch_slop, ..window.gesture_tuning() }`). Adds a window test with one row per case (not
retuned, retuned mid-touch with a 12 px and a 6 px travel).

Why: a window reads its tuning once, when it opens (patch 0014), and its recognizer is private, so neither a
Parallax crate nor an extension trait can reach it. Android reports its device touch slop
(`ViewConfiguration.getScaledTouchSlop()`, which Flutter's FlutterView sends as `physicalTouchSlop` with every
viewport metrics update) and that value changes with the display density while the app's one window stays open for
the life of the process; without this patch a density change could reach only windows opened later, of which
Android has none. No cfg: the setter is core and the same on every platform.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

